# DTF tier manifest: repo-mutation-authz  (#2603 G1 — mutation authz choke-point)
# Profile-set: filesystem / single. The write-authorization gap is in the HTTP
# auth layer (native `repo_visibility_middleware`, REST `require_repo_action`),
# ahead of any storage backend, so it is storage-independent.
#
# The bug (#2603 G1, HIGH): repository mutation authorization conflated
# *visibility* / *coarse membership* with an *action grant*. On a PUBLIC
# repository that carries no fine-grained permission rules, the native format
# write path skipped its membership branch entirely (`else if !is_public`), so
# ANY authenticated caller could PUT/POST/DELETE artifacts — cross-tenant write
# / supply-chain poisoning. The REST + chunked-upload + OCI paths shared the
# same `is_public -> Ok` / rules-less fall-through. The fix routes every
# mutation through the single canonical `check_repository_action` choke-point,
# DENY-BY-DEFAULT: `is_public` confers a READ baseline only and never a write.
#
# Oracle (discriminating, RELEASE_GATE=1), on a PUBLIC rules-less maven repo:
#   (CONTROL)  a developer-role MEMBER native maven PUT -> 201 (legit push
#              unchanged; public read below stays 200 too).
#   (BOUNDARY) an authenticated NON-member native maven PUT -> 403 (GREEN). On
#              the vulnerable baseline this returns 201/200 (RED — the write
#              SUCCEEDS on a repo the caller has no write grant on). A DB assert
#              confirms the non-member PUT persisted NO artifacts row on the fix
#              (0) vs one on baseline.
#   (READ)     anonymous GET of the member-pushed artifact -> 200 (the public
#              read baseline is intact; the fix restricts writes only).
#
# Git-LFS download-vs-upload discriminator (PUBLIC rules-less gitlfs repo, same
# authenticated NON-member caller for both). The git-lfs `POST /objects/batch`
# is a write HTTP method but the DOWNLOAD batch is the mandatory `git lfs pull`
# negotiation (a read); only the UPLOAD batch is a write. A method-only mutation
# gate 403s the whole POST and breaks download negotiation for read-only members
# and public-repo non-members:
#   (LFS-DOWNLOAD) non-member download batch -> 200 (GREEN). The over-restrictive
#              method-only gate returns 403 (RED); the pre-#2603 baseline never
#              gated batch at all. The fix classifies the batch POST as
#              non-mutating so the handler self-gates.
#   (LFS-UPLOAD)   non-member upload batch -> 403 (GREEN). The batch handler
#              self-gates uploads through the same deny-by-default action
#              choke-point, so the download exemption opens no upload hole
#              (a hole would return 200, RED). download 200 vs upload 403 for the
#              same caller/repo is the discriminator.
#
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false: the oracle drives an admin login + two user creates
# + two logins + a repo create + several back-to-back native PUT/GETs; the base
# default (ON) would throttle them. A tier that must disable a shipped control
# does so explicitly (design 2.2, as isolation/5f2q-upload-scope do).
PROFILES="storage.filesystem"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
